LEAD = {
    "headline": "A CREATOR SPENT THREE YEARS AND ABOUT 50 ATTEMPTS PUBLICLY HUNTING A COPY OF A DISCONTINUED TOM FORD EYESHADOW, TOM FORD NEVER ANSWERED HER, AND THE RIVAL THAT DID SOLD OUT AT SEPHORA IN 12 HOURS AND LIFTED SALES OF ITS OTHER SHADES BY 305 PERCENT",
    "deck": "Hannah Chody was not pitching anybody.  She was documenting a search for 288,000 followers, and in doing so she ran three years of free product development in public.  Saie read it as a brief, flew her to Italy, and shipped the thing.",
    "stamps": [
        ("MARKETING BREW  12 NOV", "https://www.marketingbrew.com/stories/saie-bellini-tom-ford-dupe-hannah-chody-creator-brand-strategy"),
        ("HANNAH CHODY ON TIKTOK", "https://www.tiktok.com/@hannahchody"),
        ("SAIE SUPERSUEDE BAKED EYESHADOW", "https://saiehello.com/products/supersuede-baked-eyeshadow"),
    ],
    "body": [
        "Tom Ford Beauty discontinued a cream and powder eyeshadow in the shade Golden Peach in 2023.  Hannah Chody, a creator with <mark>more than 288,000 TikTok followers</mark>, has spent the three years since trying to find a replacement on camera.  Marketing Brew reported this morning that she has tested <mark>around 50 candidates</mark> across that search, all of it documented publicly.  She told Allure in 2024 that she had contacted Tom Ford Beauty and never heard back: \"I have tried to get in touch to no avail, but I am here.  I am patiently waiting.\"",
        "Saie, a smaller beauty brand, was watching, because Chody's own commenters kept tagging it.  At that point Saie did not sell an eyeshadow at all.  When it decided to enter the category, founder and chief executive Laney Crowell went straight to Chody with an unusual offer.  In Crowell's words: \"I said, I know this is kind of fast timing, but I am actually going to Italy to where we make the product.  Do you want to come with, and we can actually develop the shade in person?\"  Chody said yes immediately, and spent <mark>about a month</mark> and several rounds of product testing helping create and name the shade.  It is called Bellini.",
        "The numbers Saie gave Marketing Brew.  <mark>More than 7,500 people joined the waitlist before launch</mark>.  When it went live on Sephora's website it <mark>sold out in 12 hours</mark>.  <mark>More than 1,300 people have since signed up for restock alerts</mark>.  And the part that should make you sit up: Crowell says the launch produced a halo on the rest of the range, <mark>driving a 305 percent sales lift across Saie's other core shades</mark>.  A Sephora-hosted TikTok Shop livestream around it <mark>set a record for the highest unit sales of any featured Sephora brand to date</mark>, by Sephora's own account, and generated <mark>6.5 million impressions, 89 percent of them from new viewers</mark>.",
        "Here is what actually happened, stripped of the beauty-industry framing.  For three years a creator ran a public, unpaid, iterative product development programme with a live audience attached to it.  She defined the specification.  She tested the market roughly fifty times and published every result.  She proved demand by having thousands of strangers tell her they wanted the same thing.  Then she sat there, visibly, waiting for someone to pick it up.  Tom Ford Beauty owned the original formula, the nostalgia and the trademark, and left the finished brief lying in the open for three years.  Saie picked it up, and the expensive half of the work was already done.",
        "Notice which asset mattered.  Saie did not buy Chody's reach, although it used it.  It bought a demand signal nobody else had bothered to read, plus the one person whose community had been following the problem long enough to care about the solution.  Crowell's framing of the lesson is the whole thing: listening to a community \"especially in areas where competitors may not be\" doing anything.  Her sign-off is tidier than most agency positioning decks.  \"You say it, we create it.\"",
        "One honest caveat on repeatability, and Crowell volunteers it herself rather than being pressed on it.  \"There was a magic to this.  It is not going to be easily replicable.\"  She is right that you cannot manufacture a three-year public search.  What you can do is go and find the ones already running, because there are hundreds of them in every category and almost nobody is reading them.",
    ],
    "numbers": [("12 HRS", "to sell out at Sephora"), ("305%", "sales lift on other shades"), ("7,500+", "waitlist before launch")],
    "flagnote": "Every performance figure here comes from Saie, with the livestream record attributed to Sephora, and none of it has been independently measured.  No spend, no creator fee and no unit volumes are disclosed anywhere, and a sell-out depends entirely on how much stock was made.  The 305 percent lift is a brand-reported figure over an unstated period, and the restock demand means the product was out of stock, so some of that lift could be shoppers buying an adjacent shade because the one they wanted was gone.  Tom Ford Beauty has not commented.  Chody's 2024 statement that she contacted the brand is her own account.",
    "so_what": "The valuable thing a creator can hand you is not always an audience.  Sometimes it is a finished product brief with three years of demand testing already attached, published in public, free to read.  Chody did the specification work, the iteration and the market validation, and the brand that won was simply the first one to treat her feed as research rather than as media.  Tom Ford had every structural advantage and lost because nobody there was reading.",
    "do_this": "Spend one afternoon this week searching your own product category for the words dupe, discontinued, bring back and alternative, and write down every creator running a long public search with an engaged comment section underneath it.  Then contact the best one with a product question rather than a campaign brief, and ask what they would change.",
}

